register(ArtifactSkip);
define
    /*  THE NIT, LIVE. Read a child's isRulE the ONLY way that discriminates --
        from inside an action, on the member, captured with := -- and show that
        a real term and a minted artifact answer differently.  */
    asProbe code={
        asR := isRulE;
        $$asR;
        asN := noPrinT;
        $$asN;
        cerr "  child " taG " isRulE " asR " noPrinT " asN:;
        };
    asWalk code={
        iterate asCur on argument;
        while ++asCur;
            asProbe(*asCur);
        };
    asRun code={
        cerr "ARTIFACTSKIP -- children of NumbeR after setParse":;
        setParse(NumbeR);
        asWalk(NumbeR);
        fixArtifactSkipHere = 0;
        };
    ;
asRun();
print "ARTIFACTSKIP SENTINEL -- reached the foot":;
stop();

artifactSkipT -- THE ARTIFACT SKIP READS THE STRUCTURAL FACT. Promoted into the
fleet 2026-09-08 from incant/fixits/artifactSkipByFlag, which is the third life
in the register's own lifecycle: born a reminder, stepped as a repro, and now
the regression test for the thing it complained about.

WHAT IT ASSERTS. The four children of NumbeR after setParse, by value:

    numberSet     isRulE 1   noPrinT 0     a real grammar term
    FloaT         isRulE 1   noPrinT 0     a real grammar term
    builtinParsE  isRulE 0   noPrinT 1     a minted artifact
    builtinActoR  isRulE 0   noPrinT 1     a minted artifact

THE RULING IT CERTIFIES. walkRules used to skip an artifact by reading noPrinT,
so noPrint carried two meanings at once -- "do not print me" and "I am an
artifact, not a grammar term". It gates on `isRulE == 0` now. The structural
fact was always there; nothing had to be added and no new flag was minted.

/  ASSERTABILITY, NOT BEHAVIOUR, and this is the honest reading. The two gates
AGREE on today's data: parser walks 21 rules either way and WITNESS reads 1
either way. They can only disagree for a node that is a noPrint RULE or a
printing ARTIFACT, and neither exists right now. That agreement is exactly why
noPrint appeared to work, and it is why the fix changes what is being READ
rather than what happens. The rows pin the fact so the day those populations
diverge the fleet says so instead of anyone guessing.

/  AND THE GATE IS `== 0`, NEVER `!isRulE`. MEASURED, and the obvious spelling
is the broken one:

    if !isRulE;      builtinParsE VISITED   builtinActoR VISITED    skips nothing
    if isRulE == 0;  builtinParsE SKIPPED   builtinActoR SKIPPED    correct

`!` on a zero-holding field answers false -- the defect pinned as andProbe
AP-5b -- so the bang form produces a walker that silently visits every artifact
at exit 0 while looking entirely reasonable. Do not tidy this into a bang.

/  WHY THE PROBE READS FROM INSIDE AN ACTION, captured with `:=`, on the member.
Three earlier shapes were void: a dotted read through an iterate cursor returns
the CURSOR (bear-trap 35); `@grup` re-points at the cursor rather than the
member; and `if isRulE` tests EXISTENCE, so it read 1 on every child including
the artifacts (bear-trap 26 payment 2). And `=` would capture nothing at all --
it must be `:=` (bear-trap 41), or every column reads a tag echo.
